{"type": "thinking", "text": "With Math *off*, output \"A x^2 B and Costs 5 and 10 C\" — all dollar signs stripped. If math off, this is definitely wrong: `$x^2$` should show literally, and `$5...$10` should show dollar signs. So the converter is still applying math-style dollar handling despite the setting being off. But maybe the setting isn't loaded due to static cache; to be sure, restart process.\n\nActually wait: perhaps the Math setting isn't a \"toggle\" but tapping it opened... no.\n\nHmm, could the setting have been \"off\" all along and the checkbox we saw was actually default? Let's think: At first visit, after tapping row, CheckBox [checked] appeared. That means before tapping it was unchecked, and tapping *enabled* it. Wait! Initially the row showed no CheckBox in the hierarchy. CheckBoxes in Android may only appear when... no. Hmm, in this harness, maybe unchecked checkbox isn't exposed as element, and tapping the row *checked* it, adding the CheckBox element. If so, we ENABLED math, not disabled! Then when tapping again, checkbox disappeared => disabled. Let me re-evaluate:\n\nFirst Markdown settings observe:\n[6] LinearLayout contains \"Math\"... \n(no checkbox)\nThen we tapped 6, resulting:\n[7] CheckBox id=checkbox [checked]\nSo tapping the row enabled math. Initially math was OFF. Then we tapped again and the checkbox disappeared => math OFF again. So our test with Money in between was with math ON. That explains the $ stripping! Then after turning off, Money still stripped \"$\"... Wait timeline:\n\n1. Initial Markdown settings: no checkbox => math OFF (default).\n2. We tapped Math row → checkbox [checked] => math ON.\n3. Then we tapped checkbox [7] → disappeared => math OFF.\n4. Then navigated and viewed Money → \"Costs 5 and 10\" (math should be OFF). Hmm, that contradicts... unless in step 1, no checkbox means checked (checked state hides check box? no).\n\nHold on, need clarity about how observe represents checkboxes. In file sort dialog, \"Reverse order\" showed no \"checked\" initially and had CheckedTextView. When we tapped it, `[clickable,checked]` appeared. So checked state is represented as attribute, not by absence of element. For settings, the CheckBox element appears only after tap—possibly because it's a CheckBox view that is initially unchecked but still present in hierarchy normally (should be listed as \"CheckBox id=checkbox\" without checked). It wasn't listed initially. Odd. Maybe the harness snapshot truncated? It listed 11 elements; row, etc. The CheckBox might have been hidden because the setting row uses a custom widget where checkbox is only added when checked? Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, maybe tapping the row toggled from checked to unchecked, and the CheckBox was present initially but our first observation missed it because... The second observation after tap includes CheckBox [checked] which implies after tap it is checked. If toggling from initial we don't know.\n\nLet's directly determine now: currently after our toggles, observe shows no CheckBox => unchecked or hidden. We can tap and observe; if checkbox [checked] appears, then tapping enables (i.e., off→on). Then we know initial state (no checkbox). Let's test carefully, since we need restore default (likely math ON? unsure). The default in Markor is Math OFF I believe (KaTeX adds ~ big lib, default off). But this environment might have it on.\n\nWe can determine by whether view rendering of `$x^2$` changes after toggling! Great: current state (no checkbox) rendered `$` stripped. If that means math ON with hidden checked state, then tapping (checking visible?) might turn it off and rendering would show dollars. Let's do: go to settings, tap Math (observe checkbox appears), return and view QAtmp. Compare.\n\nActually simpler: use the current QAtmp and toggle. Let's go to settings and tap Math once (checkbox will appear), then back to QAtmp view (via Files > QAtmp; remember view mode). Then see rendering.\n\nLet me do."}
