{"type": "thinking", "text": "Whoa. With regex ON, replacing `cat(@home)` with `$1` step replace produced \"$1 dog@home\" — the replacement is literal \"$1\", NOT the group \"@home\". Is that expected? In regex mode, replacement usually supports $1. Let's compare with Replace all. If Replace all produces \"@home dog@home\" while step produces \"$1 dog@home\", then there's an inconsistency and likely a bug in step replace (the changed area!). The changelog says \"step-by-step replace action now passes its output through a plain-string path\" — maybe this intentional change broke regex group substitution. Hmm, \"passes its output through a plain-string path\" — if they quote replacement, then $1 is literal even in regex mode, which would be a regression. Let's test Replace all.\n\nUndo first (close search, undo), then redo with replace all. Or just use Replace all here on remaining text? Search no longer matches (\"cat(@home)\"), so replace-all would do nothing. Need undo. Close search > undo."}
