alue. expected: \"With Use Regular Expression enabled, replacing the match of `cat(@home)` with `$1` in the step-by-step Replace (1) action should insert the captured group `@home`, matching Replace all.\"\nobserved: \"Replace (1) inserted the literal text `$1`, producing `$1 dog@home`; Replace all on the same search/replacement produced `@home dog@home`.\"\n\nSteps list. element_ref: need latest observe where editor shows \"@home dog@home\" element 26. But the observed defect quote \"$1 dog@home\" was on screen earlier in latest observe? The last observe has \"@home dog@home\". The rule: quote exact text seen on screen. \"$1 dog@home\" was shown in a previous observation (element 26). element_ref from *latest* observe needs 26 (editor). The quote is from earlier but was on screen. Fine.\n\nActually to be safe, reproduce step replace again now and capture \"$1 dog@home\" fresh in latest observation before filing. Let's undo replace all first, then step replace, then observe and file. Current search dialog open, text \"@home dog@home\". Close, undo, reopen, step. But that's many steps. Alternatively file now using earlier observation—the observed quote was indeed on-screen. The instruction says \"copy it verbatim from an observation\". It was. element_ref 26 from latest observation is the editor. I think fine.\n\nHmm, but note the replacement might actually be intentional: \"plain-string path\" could mean replacement string is plain (literal) in step replace by design. But the UI still has regex mode toggle. If step replace offers regex search but literal replacement, inconsistency is still bug. Developer likely confirms.\n\nBefore filing, consider: maybe in regex mode, \"$1\" isn't supported in step replace but \"@home\" with group works via `Matcher.replaceFirst` normally. The fact replace-all gives @home proves the expectation. Good.\n\nLet's file."}
