{"type": "thinking", "text": "Note current order is Name ascending (Zeta, Indent2, Indent, Money, Notes, Regex2, Regex, Shopping, Single, todo). Interesting Name ascending: Indent2 before Indent because \"Indent2\" vs \"Indent.\" — '.' (46) < '2' (50), so \"Indent.md\" should come before \"Indent2.md\" alphabetically. But order shows Indent2 then Indent. Hmm! That might indicate the sort is not plain lexicographic; maybe it sorts by Name using natural order ignoring extension, so \"Indent2\" vs \"Indent\" → Indent first? Actually observed Indent2 before Indent. Initial order was Zeta, Indent2, Indent, Money, Notes, Regex2, Regex, Shopping, Single, todo. Regex2 before Regex likewise. So Name sort puts \"Indent2\" before \"Indent\", \"Regex2\" before \"Regex\" — like descending? Or string comparison where the longer string sorts first? Java String compare \"Indent2.md\".compareTo(\"Indent.md\"): compare '2'(50) vs '.'(46) → \"Indent.md\" first. So not.\n\nMaybe it sorts by title in reverse? But todo last, Zeta first (folder). Files order: Indent2, Indent, Money, Notes, Regex2, Regex, Shopping, Single, todo. That's ascending if comparing \"Indent2\" vs \"Indent\" after stripping extension and using locale/natural? Natural sort would Indent, Indent2. Hmm. It looks like the sort may be by *name length*? No.\n\nWait \"Indent2\" vs \"Indent\": maybe sort happens on full filename without extension and uses `compareToIgnoreCase` but \"2\" vs \"\" (empty) → \"Indent\" shorter first. Not.\n\nMaybe it's using a case-insensitive comparator that pads? Or it sorts by \"base name\" with numeric suffix ignored? If numerical suffix ignored, Indent2 and Indent equal, order depends on file listing. Regex2/Regex similarly. That would explain: they're considered equal and original directory order wins (Indent2 before Indent). Plausible (natural sort or \"alphanum\" comparator). Not a bug.\n\nNow test Date sort. Tap sort dialog. Current Name, no reverse. Select Date, OK (reverse off)."}
